1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,809
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,809 --> 00:00:07,085
This is chapter twenty: sets, queues and deques.

3
00:00:07,535 --> 00:00:10,329
List and Map get most of the attention.

4
00:00:10,329 --> 00:00:22,362
These three interfaces cover jobs that code often solves badly with a list: membership tests, deduplication, stacks, work queues, and give me the most urgent item next.

5
00:00:23,262 --> 00:00:26,178
Four jobs, each done with the wrong structure.

6
00:00:26,178 --> 00:00:29,792
Deduplicating with List dot contains, which is quadratic.

7
00:00:29,792 --> 00:00:34,484
A java dot util Stack, which is synchronised and iterates from the bottom.

8
00:00:34,484 --> 00:00:37,971
A LinkedList as a queue, allocating a node per element.

9
00:00:37,971 --> 00:00:43,043
And re-sorting a whole list on every insert, just to find the most urgent order.

10
00:00:43,043 --> 00:00:43,994
Each one works.

11
00:00:43,994 --> 00:00:50,398
Each is slower than it needs to be by an order of magnitude, and two carry correctness traps besides.

12
00:00:51,298 --> 00:00:53,615
Every Set is a Map in disguise.

13
00:00:53,615 --> 00:00:58,249
HashSet is a HashMap whose values are one shared dummy object.

14
00:00:58,249 --> 00:01:02,285
So it inherits O of one lookups and unspecified order.

15
00:01:02,735 --> 00:01:06,980
LinkedHashSet is a LinkedHashMap, so it keeps insertion order.

16
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:14,470
TreeSet is a TreeMap: log n, sorted, and it uses compareTo, not equals, to decide duplicates.

17
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:18,141
The exception is EnumSet, which is a bit vector.

18
00:01:18,141 --> 00:01:21,363
Up to sixty-four constants fit in a single long.

19
00:01:21,813 --> 00:01:23,912
Here's the actual HashSet add.

20
00:01:23,912 --> 00:01:27,271
It puts the element as a key, mapped to PRESENT.

21
00:01:27,271 --> 00:01:32,799
So everything from the HashMap chapter applies, including the mutable-key trap.

22
00:01:33,699 --> 00:01:36,766
Queue has two methods for every operation.

23
00:01:36,766 --> 00:01:41,292
Add, remove and element throw when the queue is full or empty.

24
00:01:41,292 --> 00:01:44,942
Offer, poll and peek return false or null instead.

25
00:01:45,392 --> 00:01:50,689
The split exists because empty or full is often normal, not exceptional.

26
00:01:50,689 --> 00:01:54,220
A worker draining a queue expects it to run dry.

27
00:01:54,220 --> 00:01:58,192
So use offer, poll and peek for ordinary control flow.

28
00:01:59,092 --> 00:02:04,331
To deduplicate while keeping the order things arrived in, use a LinkedHashSet.

29
00:02:04,331 --> 00:02:10,173
That's the real requirement far more often than a plain HashSet, which scrambles order.

30
00:02:10,173 --> 00:02:13,599
Stream dot distinct does the same thing internally.

31
00:02:14,049 --> 00:02:16,970
Set algebra makes reconciliation easy.

32
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:21,658
SKUs we sell but the warehouse doesn't stock is a difference.

33
00:02:21,658 --> 00:02:24,118
SKUs in both is an intersection.

34
00:02:24,568 --> 00:02:25,436
Two catches.

35
00:02:25,436 --> 00:02:29,848
These methods mutate the set you call them on, so copy first.

36
00:02:29,848 --> 00:02:36,792
And doing this with two lists of two hundred thousand SKUs would mean forty billion comparisons.

37
00:02:37,692 --> 00:02:40,977
TreeSet's real superpower is neighbour queries.

38
00:02:40,977 --> 00:02:44,752
Ceiling gives the next slot at or after twelve thirty.

39
00:02:44,752 --> 00:02:48,246
Floor gives the latest one that's already started.

40
00:02:48,246 --> 00:02:50,833
subSet gives a range, as a live view.

41
00:02:50,833 --> 00:02:51,811
Each is log n.

42
00:02:51,811 --> 00:02:54,887
A HashSet would have to check every element.

43
00:02:55,337 --> 00:03:02,060
So choose TreeSet for these queries, not for sorted iteration, which you could get by sorting once.

44
00:03:02,060 --> 00:03:09,395
And remember: it uses compareTo, so an element that compares equal to one already there is silently dropped.

45
00:03:10,295 --> 00:03:14,542
ArrayDeque is the right answer for both a stack and a queue.

46
00:03:14,542 --> 00:03:18,009
Push and pop for last-in, first-out undo history.

47
00:03:18,009 --> 00:03:22,114
Offer and poll for first-in, first-out pending dispatches.

48
00:03:22,564 --> 00:03:30,860
Compared to the alternatives: Stack synchronises every method, and iterates from the bottom, the opposite of pop order.

49
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:34,555
LinkedList costs about twenty-four bytes per element.

50
00:03:34,555 --> 00:03:36,507
ArrayDeque costs about four.

51
00:03:36,507 --> 00:03:39,225
The one rule: ArrayDeque rejects nulls.

52
00:03:40,125 --> 00:03:43,531
PriorityQueue is a min-heap by its comparator.

53
00:03:43,531 --> 00:03:45,975
Poll always returns the smallest.

54
00:03:45,975 --> 00:03:49,899
So for the most urgent first, reverse the comparator.

55
00:03:49,899 --> 00:03:57,896
Use it when items arrive over time and you repeatedly need the next most urgent one, at log n per operation.

56
00:03:58,346 --> 00:04:02,950
And here's a classic: the top ten orders by value, from a million.

57
00:04:02,950 --> 00:04:04,903
Keep a min-heap of size ten.

58
00:04:04,903 --> 00:04:08,949
After each insert, if it holds eleven, evict the smallest.

59
00:04:08,949 --> 00:04:13,483
That's n log k time and k memory, instead of sorting all million.

60
00:04:13,483 --> 00:04:18,506
Just remember to sort the ten at the end, because the heap isn't sorted.

61
00:04:19,406 --> 00:04:23,287
Inside PriorityQueue is a binary heap stored in an array.

62
00:04:23,287 --> 00:04:28,530
The only invariant is that each parent is less than or equal to its children.

63
00:04:28,530 --> 00:04:33,161
So the root is the minimum, but siblings are in no particular order.

64
00:04:33,161 --> 00:04:41,059
That's why iterating it gives heap order, which looks nearly sorted on small inputs and clearly isn't on large ones.

65
00:04:41,509 --> 00:04:43,806
ArrayDeque is a circular buffer.

66
00:04:43,806 --> 00:04:51,412
Two indices move around a fixed array and wrap at the end, so both ends are O of one with nothing shifted.

67
00:04:51,412 --> 00:04:54,928
It forbids nulls because a null slot means empty.

68
00:04:55,378 --> 00:04:58,822
And watch for PriorityQueue's linear operations.

69
00:04:58,822 --> 00:05:03,056
Remove of an object and contains both scan the whole array.

70
00:05:03,056 --> 00:05:08,797
If dispatches often get cancelled, mark them and skip on poll, or use a TreeSet.

71
00:05:09,697 --> 00:05:10,910
Production rules.

72
00:05:10,910 --> 00:05:16,693
Use a Set for membership and deduplication, and LinkedHashSet when order matters.

73
00:05:16,693 --> 00:05:19,191
Copy before retainAll or removeAll.

74
00:05:19,191 --> 00:05:28,043
Use TreeSet for neighbour queries, ArrayDeque for every stack and queue, a size-k min-heap for top-k, and EnumSet for enums.

75
00:05:28,493 --> 00:05:32,487
Never use java dot util Stack, or LinkedList as a queue.

76
00:05:32,487 --> 00:05:38,692
Never iterate a PriorityQueue expecting sorted order, or call its remove in a hot loop.

77
00:05:38,692 --> 00:05:40,974
Never put null in an ArrayDeque.

78
00:05:40,974 --> 00:05:44,326
And never share a deque or heap across threads.

79
00:05:44,326 --> 00:05:48,248
That's what blocking queues are for, in chapter thirty.

80
00:05:49,148 --> 00:05:49,788
To recap.

81
00:05:49,788 --> 00:05:54,764
Every Set is a Map underneath, with the same costs and the same traps.

82
00:05:54,764 --> 00:05:58,602
Use offer, poll and peek when empty or full is normal.

83
00:05:58,602 --> 00:06:02,867
ArrayDeque is the answer for stacks and queues, never Stack.

84
00:06:02,867 --> 00:06:07,559
And PriorityQueue is a min-heap: poll is ordered, iteration isn't.

85
00:06:08,009 --> 00:06:11,163
Next is chapter twenty-one, sequenced collections.

86
00:06:11,163 --> 00:06:12,425
Thanks for watching.
